    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48711.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I am. I mean, you know, I've been telling people for months now that the presidency is too close to call that the Republicans would likely take the Senate and the Democrats would likely take the House. Now, two of those things have happened. Well, you know, I didn't call the presidency, but I did not expect the win Donald Trump's win to be so big. And you know, I've got my personal feelings about that. You know, it's a historical thing when a guy who has said what he said uh... and done what he'd done get it gets get to reelected and obviously have my feelings about that uh... but it was big yeah i mean don't want me when the popular vote in this country and i think that that's something we all got to think a lot about connecticut of course connecticut of course resisted that along with massachusetts new hampshire in vermont new england remains a Uh, you know, I would say a very, you know, thoughtful, um, uh, Democratic, uh, whether you put those two things together, like your last caller or not, you know, uh, Connecticut feels like a little bit of an oasis right now because MAGA was, uh, roundly rejected here, um, at every level, but even in Connecticut, you know, we had a couple of local races that we were optimistic about, a couple of state Senate races that we were optimistic about and, uh, they didn't, they didn't pan out. So it's, uh, it's a sobering morning. No question.",
